// Address layout for x86_64 user programs and servers.
//
// Programs: musl-linked binaries land in the high user carve-out
// at VA 0x60000000 (matches aarch64 — same `slix-prog.ld` script).
// USP = 0x60090000 puts the initial SP at the top of the 16-page
// stack region (0x60080000..0x60090000) that vm_create_process_pt
// installs under PD[256] of PDPT[1]. The legacy 0xCC000..0xFFFFF
// region still exists for native sysl programs but isn't used as
// a stack anymore.
//
// Servers: code at 0xD4000, 32KB stack. Servers have large BSS
// (RS ~128KB) and need ~176KB to 0x100000.
//
// PHASE 1 REPLACEMENT TARGET (master roadmap invariant 1):
// PROG_USP/PROG_SSP as fixed addresses do not survive the VMA
// rewrite. Once Phase 1 lands, every process gets a per-process
// VMA list and stacks are allocated from a stack-VMA region by
// the kernel; the initial SP comes back from `mmap`-equivalent
// machinery, not a constant. Until then, this is the intentional
// bring-up shape — do not add new fixed VAs here, route any new
// per-process region through the would-be VMA layer instead.

module oskit.arch

val PROG_USP = 0x60090000
val PROG_SSP = 0x60080000
val SRV_USP  = 0xD4000
val SRV_SSP  = 0xD3000

// AT_PLATFORM string for the SysV initial stack auxv. ld-musl reads
// this via `__init_libc` -> `__libc.platform` and uses it to pick
// HWCAP-gated string routines. Must match what musl was built for —
// we cross-build x86_64-slix-linux-musl, so the platform name is
// the unprefixed arch name.
val PLATFORM_NAME = "x86_64"
